PROBE 3 — G1 anchor range vs the rates the sweep actually uses
==========================================================================
    anchor covers b=[1, 2, 3, 4, 5]; sweep uses b=[1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8]; unanchored: [6, 8]
    b=8 attempted reconstruction of the pre-fix path = 4.1267067e-05
    b=8 corrected (direct integration)               = 4.1267054e-05
    -> reconstruction error +0.000% — DOES NOT reproduce the documented +16%; the defect depended on more than the identity itself, so this probe proves nothing about that specific value
    b=8 historical value per RESULTS.md              = 4.7910000e-05 (+16.1%)
    b=8 Panter-Dite asymptote 2.7207*2^-16           = 4.1514587e-05
    ratio to Shannon: true 2.704, historical 3.140, bound 2.7207 -> Panter-Dite would reject the historical value, and the true value clears it by only 0.6%

  ==> G1 published-table anchor: BLIND
      The anchor covers b<=5; the sweep runs to 8, so b=6 and b=8 carry no assertion at all — that part is structural and certain. The consequence is not merely a missing check: the scalar MSE is G3's threshold, so an inflated value makes the one check guarding axis C strictly MORE permissive. Panter-Dite (2.7207*2^-2b) bounds the unanchored rates and is currently printed as a NOTE rather than asserted; it would reject the historical value (ratio 3.140 > 2.7207). Caveat on this probe: the attempt above to reconstruct the historical defect failed (+0.000%), so the range gap is demonstrated and the specific value is taken from the record.

